# Phase 22 - Typography, Color, Spacing, Radius, and Token System Audit

Date: 2026-06-26
Scope: global token facade, product visual tokens, foundation tokens, semantic utility layer, core controls/surfaces, static hard-value distribution.
Goal lens: refined, breathable, simple. No hardcoding, no temporary CSS pressure, no page-by-page visual overrides.
Benchmark lens: X, Threads, Instagram, and Apple-style product systems: quiet neutral surfaces, few accent colors, stable type ramp, predictable spacing rhythm, semantic component contracts.
Evidence type: static source and style-contract audit. This phase does not claim fresh rendered browser validation.

## Files and evidence inspected

- `src/index.css`
- `src/styles/layers/foundation.css`
- `src/styles/00-tokens.css`
- `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css`
- `src/styles/tokens/foundation.css`
- `src/styles/tokens/theme.css`
- `src/styles/system/ui-design-contract-tokens.css`
- `src/styles/02-core.css`
- `src/styles/02-core-controls.css`
- `src/styles/02-core-surfaces.css`
- `src/styles/02-core-sheets-actions.css`
- `src/styles/system/ui-foundation-clean.css`
- Static hard-value and utility-class sampling across `src/styles/**/*.css` and `src/**/*.tsx`.

## Import order evidence

Current CSS entry order is coherent:

- `src/index.css` imports Tailwind first, then `foundation`, `system-core`, `components`, `features`, and `contracts`.
- `src/styles/layers/foundation.css` imports `00-tokens.css`, then `00-product-tokens.css`, `00-profile-tokens.css`, `00-promote-tokens.css`, `ui-design-contract-tokens.css`, base, core, and clean foundation utilities.
- `src/styles/00-tokens.css` is a facade over `theme`, `foundation`, `layout-components`, `feature-contracts`, and `social-contracts`.

Design read:

- This is a good architecture. Product-level taste is intentionally layered above primitive tokens.
- The system is capable of supporting a refined product language without CSS pressure.
- The weak point is not import order. The weak point is that too many features still bypass the contract layer through raw utilities or local hard values.

## Executive summary

The typography/color/spacing foundation is better than average and already has a clear product direction: warm off-white canvas, high-contrast ink, restrained blue-violet action color, compact social-app typography, soft glass surfaces, and a 4px spacing base.

The main maturity gap is token governance. There are multiple token layers that redefine the same concepts, and feature/page code can still escape into Tailwind utilities, raw pixel values, and page-specific form/button geometry. This creates a product that can look refined on major screens, but slowly loses consistency in edge states, admin panels, auth sheets, profile forms, and promotional flows.

The next design-system step should be a `Visual Token Governance Contract`: decide which token layer is canonical for color, typography, spacing, radius, shadow, motion, breakpoint, and component geometry, then force page/feature code to consume semantic tokens only.

## Overall health

Score: 7.3 / 10

Strengths:

- Product tokens clearly document the intent: components should consume product tokens instead of redefining raw values locally in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:1-8`.
- Product palette is restrained: canvas `#F8F8F7`, ink `#1A1A1A`, muted ink `#505057/#707079`, action color `#4F5FD9`, tint `#EEF0FC` in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:17-24`.
- The type ramp is compact and appropriate for Chinese social content: `--ui-text-xs`, `--ui-text-sm`, `--ui-text-md`, `--ui-text-base`, `--ui-text-body`, `--ui-text-title`, `--ui-text-page-title` in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:67-76`.
- Global layout rhythm exists: page padding, content max width, main gap, app page gap, secondary page gap in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:139-148`.
- Theme aliases map Tailwind variables back to product tokens in `src/styles/tokens/theme.css:3-33`.
- Semantic utility classes exist for text, backgrounds, borders, type roles, section cards, buttons, chips, inputs, tabbar, and media frames in `src/styles/system/ui-foundation-clean.css:27-270`.

Primary risks:

- Foundation tokens and product tokens define overlapping concepts with different values.
- TSX files still contain large amounts of spacing/color/type utility classes.
- Feature tokens still include many raw px/rem values that are not always derived from the spacing scale.
- Admin and fallback surfaces continue to use Tailwind color/radius/spacing utilities, which bypass the refined product language.

## P0 findings

### P0-1 - Token layers redefine the same visual concepts without an explicit ownership contract

Evidence:

- Primitive foundation defines a blue brand and pale blue canvas: `--ui-brand: #287dfa`, `--ui-surface-page: #f5f8ff`, plus blue text/line/surface values in `src/styles/tokens/foundation.css`.
- Product tokens redefine the product to a warmer neutral canvas and blue-violet action color: `--ui-product-canvas: #F8F8F7`, `--ui-product-ink: #1A1A1A`, `--ui-product-brand: #4F5FD9` in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:17-24`.
- Product tokens then remap `--ui-brand`, `--ui-text-*`, `--ui-surface-*`, and `--ui-line-*` in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:35-118`.
- Import order makes product tokens win today, but the same semantic names are still defined in several places.

Why this hurts refinement:

- The visual language depends on import order rather than explicit ownership.
- New developers can accidentally consume foundation-era tokens or Tailwind colors and reintroduce a colder blue/system look.
- It becomes hard to answer which token is authoritative for a design decision.

Recommendation:

- Document and enforce token ownership tiers:
  - Primitive tier: raw scale only, no product intent.
  - Product tier: canonical brand, surface, text, line, radius, shadow, typography.
  - Contract tier: component/feature aliases only, no new raw colors unless a domain requires it.
  - Feature tier: may compose tokens, not define a new aesthetic.
- Move foundation-era brand/surface values behind neutral primitive names or mark them legacy.
- Add a stylelint or custom lint rule: feature/component CSS cannot define new hex/rgb values unless the file is an approved token file.

Acceptance gate:

- One document or lintable manifest states which files may define raw color, type, spacing, radius, shadow, motion, and breakpoint values.
- Components and features consume semantic tokens only.

### P0-2 - TSX utility classes are still the biggest threat to typography/color/spacing consistency

Evidence:

Static utility occurrence sampling by file showed the highest TSX utility concentrations:

- `src/features/admin/AdminPage.tsx`: 1842 utility-like matches.
- `src/features/admin/AdminFiltersPanel.tsx`: 328 utility-like matches.
- `src/features/profile/ProfileMobilePage.tsx`: 108 utility-like matches.
- `src/features/promote/promoteComponents.tsx`: 83 utility-like matches.
- `src/features/admin/AdminChatPanel.tsx`: 61 utility-like matches.
- `src/App.tsx`: 33 utility-like matches.

Concrete examples from sampling:

- `src/features/admin/AdminFiltersPanel.tsx` uses repeated `w-full h-11 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl px-4 text-sm font-bold outline-none`.
- `src/features/profile/ProfileMobilePage.tsx` uses repeated `w-full rounded-2xl border border-transparent ui-bg-muted px-4 py-3.5 text-base font-medium ui-text-strong`.
- `src/App.tsx` uses fallback state utilities like `text-xl font-black`, `rounded-full`, `p-4`, `h-11`, and `px-5`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Tailwind utilities are not inherently bad, but here they bypass the product token contract.
- Typography hierarchy becomes local: `text-sm/font-bold` can mean different visual priority in admin, profile, auth, and fallback states.
- Spacing becomes local: `px-4 py-3.5` is not the same as a named input/control density.
- Color becomes local: `bg-white`, `border-gray-200`, and `text-gray-950` do not respect the warmer product canvas and ink system.

Recommendation:

- Prioritize TSX utility migration by concentration:
  1. AdminPage and AdminFiltersPanel.
  2. ProfileMobilePage form and security sections.
  3. promoteComponents payment/checkout surfaces.
  4. App fallback states.
- Convert repeated utility groups into semantic primitives: `AdminInput`, `AdminPanel`, `ProfileField`, `SecurityField`, `PaymentPanel`, `CenteredStateAction`.
- Ban direct Tailwind color utilities in product UI except for approved prototypes or migration-only files.

Acceptance gate:

- Top 10 TSX utility-heavy files have a migration plan and a primitive owner.
- No new page-level form field should be built from raw `px`, `py`, `rounded`, `text`, `font`, `bg`, or `border` utility stacks.

### P0-3 - Typography has a good ramp, but semantic type roles are not enforced everywhere

Evidence:

- Product text ramp: `--ui-text-2xs: 0.6875rem`, `--ui-text-xs: 0.75rem`, `--ui-text-sm: 0.8125rem`, `--ui-text-md: 0.875rem`, `--ui-text-base: 1rem`, `--ui-text-body: 1rem`, `--ui-text-title: 1rem`, `--ui-text-page-title: 1.125rem`.
- Foundation also defines a different base/body ramp where `--ui-text-base` and `--ui-text-body` are `0.875rem`.
- `ui-foundation-clean.css` defines semantic roles: `.ui-type-caption`, `.ui-type-meta`, `.ui-type-body`, `.ui-type-title` in `src/styles/system/ui-foundation-clean.css:71-99`.
- Base CSS still remaps Tailwind-like typography classes such as `.font-medium`, `.font-semibold`, `.font-bold`, `.font-black`, `.text-lg`, `.text-xl`, `.text-meta`, `.text-brand`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- The system has both semantic roles and utility compatibility aliases.
- Pages can choose `text-sm font-bold` instead of `ui-type-meta` or `ui-type-title`, which erodes hierarchy.
- A refined social product needs fewer type decisions per page, not more.

Recommendation:

- Define canonical type roles: `display`, `page-title`, `section-title`, `body`, `body-compact`, `meta`, `caption`, `control`, `badge`, `number`.
- Map each role to size, line-height, weight, color default, and allowed emphasis states.
- Treat Tailwind-like `.text-*` and `.font-*` compatibility classes as transitional only.

Acceptance gate:

- Common components expose `typeRole` or use semantic class names internally.
- Static checks flag new `text-sm`, `text-base`, `font-bold`, `font-black`, and arbitrary `text-[...]` usage outside approved migration zones.

### P0-4 - Spacing scale is clean, but feature geometry still uses bespoke rem/px values

Evidence:

- Foundation spacing scale is a clear 4px rhythm: `--ui-space-1: 4px` through `--ui-space-8: 32px`.
- Product layout tokens use that scale for page padding, content width, page gaps, and secondary page gaps.
- Feature token sampling found many bespoke values such as:
  - `--post-create-input-padding: 0.95rem 1rem 0.72rem`.
  - `--post-create-media-padding: 0.9rem 1rem 0.98rem`.
  - `--post-create-sync-label-inset: 0.625rem`.
  - `--post-create-channel-gap: 0.375rem`.
  - `--post-create-contact-save-min-width: 112px`.
  - `--ui-promote-category-chip-max: 260px`.
  - `--ui-promote-category-price-max-width: 5.25rem`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Bespoke values are sometimes needed for optical balance, but too many make the rhythm hard to tune globally.
- Rem/px values inside feature contracts become a second spacing system.
- Apple-like polish depends on optical tuning, but the tuning should be named and reusable, not buried in one feature.

Recommendation:

- Classify feature values into three buckets:
  - `scale-derived`: rewrite as `calc(var(--ui-space-*) ...)`.
  - `component-geometry`: promote to component contract tokens.
  - `optical-exception`: keep raw value but document why it exists.
- For post-create and promote, create explicit `ComposeGeometry` and `PromoteChoiceGeometry` contracts.

Acceptance gate:

- Feature token files should have no unexplained raw rem/px values.
- Every non-scale value has a semantic token name that describes the optical reason.

### P0-5 - Color governance is good at product level, weak in feature/Admin escape zones

Evidence:

- Product tokens centralize raw product palette in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:17-24`.
- Semantic text/background utilities exist in `src/styles/system/ui-foundation-clean.css:27-49`.
- Static sampling shows Tailwind colors in admin TSX, including `bg-white`, `border-gray-200`, `bg-slate-50/70`, `text-gray-950`, `text-gray-400`, `bg-gray-950`, and `text-white`.
- Feature contract tokens also define domain-specific raw colors for admin and media, such as `--ui-admin-sidebar-surface: #171d29` and `--ui-media-muted: #eef2f6`.

Why this hurts refinement:

- Admin can intentionally have a denser professional look, but it still needs to be a designed sub-brand, not Tailwind defaults.
- Product UI can drift from warm, low-separator, surface-led composition into colder gray-blue utility panels.
- Raw color escape zones make dark/light/brand evolution expensive.

Recommendation:

- Create semantic admin palette tokens from product primitives: `--admin-surface`, `--admin-ink`, `--admin-muted`, `--admin-line`, `--admin-action`.
- Replace TSX Tailwind colors with admin semantic classes/components.
- Decide whether avatar fallback gold/dark palette is a brand asset exception or part of a reusable avatar identity contract.

Acceptance gate:

- Direct Tailwind color utilities are absent from production page/component TSX outside explicitly approved admin migration files.
- Feature raw colors exist only in token files with domain justification.

## P1 findings

### P1-1 - The product direction is strong, but too many names encode competitor references

Evidence:

- Tokens and classes include names such as `--ui-ins-link-blue`, `--ui-ins-card-*`, and comments referencing Threads-style social surface contracts.

Design read:

- Borrowing from Instagram/Threads/X is useful, and the user explicitly asked for that reference set.
- But implementation names should converge to product-owned language over time.
- Competitor-coded names make the design system feel like a clone map rather than a confident product system.

Recommendation:

- Keep benchmark references in docs.
- Rename long-term public tokens from competitor names to product roles: `social-link`, `feed-card`, `social-surface`, `compose-sheet`.
- Maintain aliases temporarily if migration risk is high.

Acceptance gate:

- New tokens do not use competitor names.
- Existing competitor aliases are marked legacy or mapped to product semantic names.

### P1-2 - Control language is close, but focus and input styles are split across multiple systems

Evidence:

- `.ui-control` defines border/background/focus behavior in `src/styles/02-core-controls.css:7-27`.
- `.ui-input` and `.ui-glass-input` define a separate input surface in `src/styles/system/ui-foundation-clean.css:190-228`.
- Profile TSX still hand-builds inputs using utility stacks rather than one input primitive.

Why this matters:

- Form focus is one of the most visible details on mobile.
- Split input systems create subtle differences in radius, padding, focus ring, surface, and line height.

Recommendation:

- Merge `ui-control`, `ui-input`, and `ui-glass-input` into a single input contract with variants: `surface`, `tone`, `density`, `withPrefix`, `invalid`.
- Profile, auth, post-create, promote, and admin should all consume that contract.

Acceptance gate:

- All text fields share one semantic component or one CSS contract.
- Error/focus/disabled states are visually consistent across auth, profile, post-create, and promote.

### P1-3 - Radius and shadow are intentionally soft, but the system needs shape taxonomy

Evidence:

- Product radius scale maps many radii back to space tokens in `src/styles/00-product-tokens.css:120-129`.
- Foundation radius scale has raw values from `4px` to `999px`.
- Section card, card, input, floating tabbar, media frame, action cell, sheet, and avatar fallback each use different radius tokens.

Why this matters:

- The visual language is soft and modern, but without taxonomy it can become “everything rounded”.
- A refined Apple-like product needs clear shape roles: page card, media, input, chip, sheet, avatar, floating control.

Recommendation:

- Define shape roles:
  - `shape-control-pill`
  - `shape-control-rounded`
  - `shape-card-soft`
  - `shape-sheet-large`
  - `shape-media-contained`
  - `shape-avatar-circle`
- Map existing tokens to roles and stop components from picking radius scale directly.

Acceptance gate:

- Components consume role tokens, not arbitrary radius scale tokens, unless they are defining the role itself.

### P1-4 - Breakpoints are scattered as raw media queries

Evidence:

Static sampling found raw breakpoint values in component/feature/system CSS:

- `390px`, `480px`, `560px`, `639px`, `640px`, `767px`, `768px`, `1023px`, and `1024px`.

Why this matters:

- These are common responsive thresholds, but they should be named contracts.
- Pixel-level layout review becomes hard when each file owns its own small-screen breakpoint.

Recommendation:

- Define breakpoint tokens or named custom media where supported by the tooling.
- Suggested names: `compact-phone`, `phone`, `tablet`, `desktop`, `wide`.
- For CSS that cannot use custom media reliably, centralize the list in a documented responsive contract and lint for unknown breakpoints.

Acceptance gate:

- No new raw breakpoint values appear outside approved responsive contract files.

## P2 findings

### P2-1 - Semantic utility layer is useful, but compatibility aliases should not become product API

Evidence:

- `.ui-text-*`, `.ui-bg-*`, `.ui-border-*`, `.ui-type-*`, `.ui-button`, `.ui-chip`, `.ui-input`, and `.ui-floating-tabbar` exist in `ui-foundation-clean.css`.
- Base CSS also maps `.font-medium`, `.font-semibold`, `.font-bold`, `.font-black`, `.text-lg`, `.text-xl`, `.text-meta`, and `.text-brand`.

Recommendation:

- Keep `.ui-*` semantic classes as public API.
- Treat Tailwind-like compatibility selectors as migration aids.
- Avoid writing new feature CSS against `.font-*` or `.text-*` selectors.

### P2-2 - Data URI placeholder is a real asset, but should be treated as asset system debt

Evidence:

- `src/styles/tokens/social-contracts.css` contains a large inline SVG data URI for `--ui-brand-media-placeholder`.

Why this matters:

- It avoids missing assets, but it makes token files noisy and difficult to review.
- The user’s “不要硬编码” constraint applies visually here too: asset content hidden inside a CSS variable is hard to govern.

Recommendation:

- Move the placeholder to a real asset file or generated asset pipeline.
- Keep the token as a URL alias only.

## Benchmark mapping

### X / Threads

Relevant principles:

- High readability, few decorative surfaces, strong text hierarchy, minimal separators.
- Feed and composer should feel consistent because users move between reading and writing constantly.

Current fit:

- Good: social surface tokens and restrained line colors support this.
- Risk: post-create bespoke geometry and TSX utility forms can diverge from feed rhythm.

### Instagram

Relevant principles:

- Media and profile hierarchy should feel crisp; icon/action systems must be instantly recognizable.
- Bottom nav and topbar should have very few surprises.

Current fit:

- Good: floating tabbar and media frame contracts exist.
- Risk: fallback/admin/profile form areas do not yet share the same visual grammar.

### Apple

Relevant principles:

- Semantic materials, predictable control sizes, calm typography, optical spacing, strong accessibility defaults.

Current fit:

- Good: safe-area awareness, font stack, compact type ramp, glass tokens, focus tokens.
- Risk: raw rem/px optical tweaks need documentation and component contracts, otherwise they become accidental rather than intentional.

## Recommended governance contracts

### Visual Token Governance Contract

Must define:

- Which files may define raw color values.
- Which files may define raw type sizes.
- Which files may define raw spacing/radius/control sizes.
- Which files may define raw motion durations and easing.
- Which files may define breakpoints.
- Which files may define feature-specific exceptions.

### Type Role Contract

Must include:

- `display`
- `page-title`
- `section-title`
- `body`
- `body-compact`
- `meta`
- `caption`
- `control`
- `badge`
- `number`

Each role should specify:

- size token
- line-height token
- weight token
- default color token
- truncation/wrapping behavior

### Spacing Rhythm Contract

Must include:

- page padding
- card padding
- row gap
- section gap
- form field gap
- control internal padding
- bottom safe area
- sheet padding
- compact phone adjustments

### Color Role Contract

Must include:

- canvas
- card
- raised surface
- muted surface
- line hairline
- line medium
- primary text
- secondary text
- muted text
- disabled text
- action primary
- action soft
- danger
- success
- warning
- overlay

### Shape Contract

Must include:

- control pill
- control rounded
- card soft
- sheet large
- media frame
- avatar circle
- badge pill

## Implementation sequence

1. Write a token ownership manifest for raw values and semantic aliases.
2. Add lint gates for raw colors in non-token CSS and Tailwind color utilities in TSX.
3. Migrate admin form/table/filter UI into admin primitives.
4. Migrate profile/security forms into shared input/control contracts.
5. Normalize post-create and promote feature geometry into component contracts.
6. Replace competitor-coded token names with product semantic aliases while keeping temporary aliases.
7. Create named responsive breakpoint contract.

## Release-readiness gates

Static gates:

- Raw hex/rgb values appear only in approved token or asset files.
- New TSX code does not add Tailwind color utilities.
- New TSX code does not add arbitrary `text-[...]`, `min-h-[...]`, `w-[...]`, or similar visual hardcoding outside migration exceptions.
- Feature CSS raw px/rem values are either scale-derived or documented as optical exceptions.
- Type roles are available for all common text usage.

Rendered gates:

- Home, create, chat, profile, sponsor, promote, recharge, history, detail, and auth states use one coherent text hierarchy.
- Profile/auth/post-create/promote forms share control height, radius, padding, focus ring, and error language.
- Admin can have its own density, but not Tailwind default gray aesthetics.
- Narrow mobile width does not create typography jumps from utility classes.

Accessibility gates:

- Text contrast should be measured against product surfaces, especially muted text on glass surfaces.
- Focus ring visibility should be checked on card, input, sheet, and bottom-nav surfaces.
- System font stack should be verified on Chinese text for weight rendering and line-height clipping.

## Current-state conclusion

The project already has a real design system foundation. It is not random CSS. The visual direction is clean, social, and restrained.

The remaining problem is enforcement. If the team keeps adding UI through TSX utility stacks and feature-specific rem/px values, the product will keep requiring pixel-level cleanup. If token ownership, type roles, spacing roles, and input/control contracts are enforced, the app can reach the requested level of refined, breathable, simple polish without CSS pressure.
